 ## Overview
-This method extends 3D Gaussian Splatting (GS) by introducing a regularization term based on the gradients of an estimated Signed Distance Function (SDF) to enforce surface-aligned geometry [S1]. While standard GS relies on photometric losses that often result in imprecise geometry and noisy normals, this approach enables high-quality surface mesh extraction and improved rendering fidelity, particularly in regions with high curvature or fine detail [S1]. It distinguishes itself from sibling methods like [Gaussian Frosting Layer Creation](12579-gaussian-frosting-layer-creation.md) and [Dual-Domain Deformation Model](12804-dual-domain-deformation-model.md) by explicitly supervising the SDF gradient direction against monocular normal estimates to ensure the Gaussians represent a coherent surface rather than a volumetric density field [G].
+This method enhances 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) by incorporating normal supervision derived from the gradients of an estimated Signed Distance Function (SDF) to improve geometric accuracy and enable high-quality mesh extraction [S1]. While standard 3DGS relies solely on photometric losses, which often result in imprecise geometry and noisy normals, this approach uses monocular normal estimates to regularize the SDF gradients, facilitating the reconstruction of surface-aligned Gaussians suitable for Poisson meshing [S1]. It distinguishes itself from sibling methods by explicitly linking the SDF gradient direction to monocular normal priors rather than relying on depth consistency or photometric stereo alone.
 
