 ### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Semi-transparent Gaussians:** Standard GS optimization may result in semi-transparent Gaussians which are undesirable for mesh extraction. The entropy regularization step is critical to force opacities towards binary values [S1].
-*   **Monocular Normal Accuracy:** The quality of the normal regularization depends on the accuracy of the pretrained monocular normal estimator (e.g., Omnidata, MiDaS) [G].
-*   **Parameter Tuning:** The weight $\lambda_r$ needs to be tuned per scene. Too high a value may over-constrain the geometry, while too low a value may not provide sufficient surface alignment [S1].
+*   **Semi-transparency:** Without entropy regularization, Gaussians may remain semi-transparent, leading to poor surface representation. The method explicitly applies entropy regularization for a short duration to force binary opacities [S1].
+*   **Monocular Normal Accuracy:** The quality of the normal supervision depends on the pretrained network (Omnidata/MiDaS). Inaccurate monocular normals may lead to incorrect gradient alignment [S1].
+*   **SDF Estimation:** The SDF is estimated based on depth differences. In regions with occlusions or large depth discontinuities, the SDF estimate may be noisy, affecting the gradient direction [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
 
 ### Time and Memory Complexity
-*   **Time Complexity:** The method adds the overhead of monocular normal estimation and SDF gradient computation to the standard GS optimization.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing additional data for SDF gradients and monocular normals. The Gaussian representation itself remains similar to standard GS [S1].
+*   **Time:** The method adds computational overhead for SDF estimation, gradient computation, and projection into camera space during each optimization step. The rasterization of gradients is efficient as it leverages the existing tile-based rasterizer [S1].
+*   **Memory:** Memory usage is similar to standard 3DGS, storing Gaussians and their parameters. The SDF is not stored explicitly, reducing memory overhead compared to grid-based SDF methods [S1].
 
 ### Measured Performance
-*   Evaluated on Mip-NeRF360, Tanks and Temples, and Deep-Blending datasets [S1].
-*   Scores higher on photorealism metrics (PSNR, SSIM) compared to other mesh-extracting rendering methods like SuGaR and Baked-SDF without compromising mesh quality [S1].
-*   On Mip-NeRF360 indoor scenes, achieves PSNR of 29.87 and SSIM of 0.921, outperforming SuGaR (PSNR 29.43, SSIM 0.910) [S1].
-*   On Mip-NeRF360 outdoor scenes, achieves PSNR of 24.61 and SSIM of 0.719, outperforming SuGaR (PSNR 24.40, SSIM 0.699) [S1].
